1. e4 d5!? { : Initial opening position for the Scandinavian Defense.
The Scandinavian Defense is one of the oldest openings in chess. In it, black immediately tries to contest the center. White's only strong response is to immediately capture the pawn, leading to the Mieses-Kotroc Variation, which highlights the main weakness of the Scandinavian Defense: that black must overextend its queen to recapture the d5 pawn. White can use this overextended queen for tempo development, giving white a stronger initial advantage. Despite being slightly theoretically unsound, the Scandinavian Defense is known for sharp, open positions that can test white players who are underprepared or during quicker time formats. Therefore, it sees some play even at high-level. } 2. exd5! { White's strongest, most common response. This immediately captures the pawn, with white asking black how it wants to recapture the pawn. } (2. e5? { White's 2nd most common response, seen more at low-level. Mistake. This declines to trade pawns in the center, avoiding much of Scandinavian Defense theory. However, this gives up white's initial tempo advantage, often transposing to either an Advanced French (2...e6) or a Caro-Kann structure where black has a tempo up (2...Bf5). }) (2. Nc3?? { White's 3rd most common response, seen more at low-level. Slight blunder. This attempts to continue to add pressure to the d5 pawn, threatening to capture it. However, black can simply advance the pawn with d4, forcing the c3 knight to move, after which the d4 pawn can be defended with e5, creating a strong center for black. }) (2. Nf3?! { Zukertort Opening, Tennison Gambit transposition. Uncommon response. Slight blunder, but can see decent practical results at low-level or quick time formats. This attempts to gambit the e4 pawn in order to trick black into falling for the ICBM trap, but this requires black to greedily accept several sacrifices and will only work against weaker opponents. Black can instead accept the gambit and carefully develop in a way that maintains the single pawn advantage, such as Bf5, avoiding the ICBM trap and maintaining the overall pawn advantage. This line will be covered in a future study by Coldfield1994. }) (2. d4?! { Blackmar-Diemer Gambit. Slight blunder, but can see decent practical results at low-level or quick time formats. This gambits the e4 pawn, intending to to trade the f-pawn for black's advanced pawn after dxe4. This leads to rapid development for white which can be enough for lower-level players to convert into a victory. However, at higher-level this simply loses a pawn and more often leads to draws or losses for white. This line will be covered in a future study by Coldfield1994. }) *


