[UTCTime "18:37:52"]

{ White immediately captures the pawn with exd4, asking black how it wants to recapture the pawn. Black has several candidate responses. }
2... Qxd5 { Mieses-Kotroc Variation. Black's most common response. This simply recaptures the pawn with the queen, centralizing a powerful piece. This comes at the cost of white being able to gain tempo development with Nc3 as the black queen is overextended and will be forced to move. } (2... Nf6 { Modern Variation. Black's 2nd most common response. Rather than immediately recapture the pawn with the queen and be forced to move it after Nc3, this chooses to add additional pressure by developing the king's side knight. White can't effectively defend this overextended pawn and often simply abandons it in favor of developing more advantageously. This response will be covered in a future study by Coldfield1994. }) (2... c6?! { Blackburne-Kloosterboer Gambit. Inaccuracy, but can see decent practical results at low-level or quick time formats. This gambits a pawn by offering a pawn trade with dxc6, leading to either the Blackburne Gambit with Nxc6 or the even more dubious Kloosterboer Gambit with e5. Both attempt to gambit at least one pawn for rapid development, which can be enough to secure victory for lower-level players. This line will be covered in a future study by Coldfield1994. }) (2... e6?! { Boehnke Gambit. Uncommon response. Mistake, but can see decent practical results at low-level or quick time formats. This attempts to gambit the e6 pawn for rapid development of black's light-squared bishop and an open d-file for black's rook after it castles queen's side. However, this gives up both of black's central pawns and overall loses a pawn, giving white a strong enough advantage to usually secure a victory. This line will be covered in a future study by Coldfield1994. }) *


