{ Black simply recaptures the pawn with Qxd5, centralizing but overextending the queen. White has several options to develop. }
3. Nc3! { White's strongest, most common response. This develops the queen's side knight by immediately attacking black's overextended queen with tempo, forcing it to move. } (3. Nf3!? { White's 2nd most common response. Inaccuracy, but can see decent practical results. This develops the king's side knight, ignoring black's centralized queen in an attempt to more quickly castle king's side and arguing that white can use the threat of Nc3 to essentially develop any minor piece with tempo. However, this allows black to pin the knight to the queen with Bg4, cramping white's development options and winning back some lost advantage from playing the Scandinavian Defense. Despite this, this can be a strong option to throw black players out of theory. This line will be covered in a future study by Coldfield1994. }) (3. d4?! { White's 3rd most common response. Inaccuracy. This immediately regains pawn control of the center, ignoring the queen and arguing that white can tempo attack the black queen with Nc3 at any later time or if the queen moves immediately then gaining control of the center is worth the tempo. However, black can immediately contest the center with e5, threatening to win the d4 pawn or create a drawish position with a pawn trade. }) (3. c4?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This tempo attacks black's overextended queen with the pawn instead of the knight in an attempt to prevent the pawn from being stuck behind the knight. However, black can tactically counterattack white's slightly exposed king with Qe4+. This forks the c2 and g2 pawns, preventing white from defending obstructing the check with either the light-squared bishop or knight without losing a pawn. If white obstructs by offering a queen trade, black can accept and argues that its pawn structure is stronger than white's and at worst will draw the position. }) (3. Qf3?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This immediately counterattacks black's aggressive opening play choice by offering a queen trade. This can be strong against low-level black players who abuse the forward position of their queen. However, black can defend its queen with Nf6, giving black a developmental advantage with a trade and a slightly stronger position without it due to white's slightly more exposed king. }) *


