[UTCTime "19:56:13"]

{ White develops its queen's side knight by immediately attacking black's overextended queen with tempo, forcing it to move. Black has several places to move the queen. }
3... Qa5! { Main Line. Black's most common, strong response. This moves the queen to an active, yet less overextended square where the queen will eventually pin white's c3 knight to its king when white continues its development by advancing the d2 pawn. } (3... Qd8! { Valencian Variation. Black's 2nd most common, strong response, seen more at low-level. This retreats the queen to its original, secure position, maintaining pressure on the d-file and arguing that white's extra development is negated by its slightly more exposed king and obstructed c2 pawn. While theoretically and practically sound at high-level, this sees slightly worse practical results at low-level compared to Qa5. }) (3... Qd6! { Gubinsky-Melts Defense. Black's 3rd most common, strong response, seen more at high-level. This retreats the queen, maintaining pressure on the d-file while still enabling the queen to exert control over the center of the board. Black argues that white's extra development is negated by its slightly more exposed king and obstructed c2 pawn. This position is slightly more active than Qd8, but less active than Qa5 while also being less secure than Qd8, yet more secure than Qa5. This variation will be covered in a future study by Coldfield1994. }) (3... Qe5+?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This attempts to recover lost tempo from needing to move the queen by checking the enemy king on a somewhat active, central square. However, white can simply obstruct the check while still continuing to develop with Be2, leaving black's queen again in position to be tempo attacked with Nf3 later. }) (3... Qe6+?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This attempts to recover lost tempo from needing to move the queen by checking the enemy king on a somewhat active, central square. However, white can simply obstruct the check while still continuing to develop with Be2. While white can't tempo attack the black queen with Nf3 like it could against Qe5+, this obstructs black's e7 pawn, forcing black to either move its queen again or slowly fianchetto its dark-squared bishop, wasting critical tempo. }) *


