{ Black moves its queen to an active, yet less overextended square with Qa5, where the queen will eventually pin white's c3 knight to its king when white continues its development by advancing the d2 pawn. White can continue development in a few ways. }
4. d4! { White's most common, strong response. This strikes out into the center, grabbing space while opening up the dark-squared bishop. This does self-pin the c3 knight to white's king, but despite this grabbing space in the center is still more valuable. This transposes with Nf3 with best play by both sides. } (4. Nf3! { White's 2nd most common, strong response. This delays grabbing space in the center with a pawn and instead develops the king's side knight, bringing white closer to castling king's side. This transposes with d4 with best play by both sides. }) (4. Bc4?! { White's 3rd most common response. Inaccuracy. This quickly develops the light-squared bishop, adding pressure to the f7 pawn and the potentially contested d5 square, while also bringing white closer to castling king's side. However, this wastes a critical tempo to needed later to pressure black's light-squared bishop after it moves to f5. } 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 Bf5 6. d4 e6 { White would be slightly better off with the f3 knight on e5 instead of the light-squared bishop on c4, as it would be able to play g4 with tempo, pressuring black's light-squared bishop away while also beginning a king's side pawn attack. } { [%csl Rg2,Be5][%cal Rg2g4,Rd1g4,Bf3e5] }) (4. d3? { White's 4th most common response. Mistake. This solidly, yet passively develops the central pawn, opening up the dark-squared bishop. However, this allows black to itself grab space in the center with e5 and self-pins the c3 knight to white's king. } 4... e5) (4. b4!? { Leonhardt Gambit. Slight blunder, but sees very good practical results below 2200. White gambits the b4 pawn in order to quickly develop the a1 rook and potentially trap black's queen. However, with best play, black holds the won pawn and regains near equality lost from playing the Scandinavian Defense. } 4... Qxb4 5. Rb1) *


