{ White strikes out into the center with d4, grabbing space while opening up the dark-squared bishop. This does self-pin the c3 knight to white's king, but despite this, grabbing space in the center is still more valuable. Black has a few different responses. }
4... c6! { (0.65@48) Black's most common, strong response. This solidly defends the d5 square, preventing white from advancing its d4 pawn easily, while also giving black's queen nice retreating squares on c7 and d8. This obstructs the b8 knight, but with white's pawn on d4, this wasn't a good option for black anyway. This transposes with Nf6 with best play by both sides. } (4... Nf6! { Mieses Variation. Black's 2nd most common, strong response, seen more at high-level. This develops the king's side knight, adding a defender to the d5 square while also bringing black closer to castling king's side. This transposes with c6 with best play by both sides. }) (4... Bf5? { Black's 3rd most common response, seen more at low-level. Mistake. This develops the light-squared bishop, putting pressure on whites c2 pawn, discouraging white from moving its queen away from its defense. However, while vague, this overextends the bishop and gives white tempo to pressure black's queen with Bd2 or add pressure to black's dark-squared bishop with Qf3, gambiting the c2 pawn which cannot be taken due to Qxb7. } 5. Bd2 (5. Qf3)) (4... Nc6?? { Black's 4th most common response, seen more at low-level. Slight blunder. This develops the queen's side knight, putting pressure on the d4 pawn. However, this obstructs the c7 pawn and encourages white to play d5, winning at least two tempo and gaining even more space in the center. } 5. d5 Nb8 { Black loses an additional tempo. } (5... Ne5 6. Bf4 { White develops with tempo. }) (5... Nb4 6. a3 Na6 7. Bb5+ Bd7 8. Bxd7+ Kxd7 { Black loses castling rights, its a6 knight is misplaced, and its king is exposed. })) (4... e5? { Anderssen Counterattack. Uncommon response. Mistake. This counterattack's white's pawn in the center, threatening to trade and weaken white's control of the center. This can see decent results at very low-level due to several simple tactics at black's disposal; however, with good play white can ignore this and simply develop with Nf3, defending the d4 pawn again, pressuring the e5 pawn, and bringing white closer to castling king's side. See Chapter X for further continuations. } 5. Nf3) *


