{ Black solidly defends the d5 square with c6, preventing white from advancing its d4 pawn easily, while also giving black's queen nice retreating squares on c7 and d8. This obstructs the b8 knight, but with white's pawn on d4, this wasn't a good option for black anyway. White can now continue development in a few ways. }
5. Nf3?! { White's most common response. Inaccuracy. This develops the king's side knight, defending the d4 pawn again and bringing white closer to castling king's side. However, despite good results at low-level, this sees worse results than Bd2 and Bc4 at high-level, as the f3 knight obstructs several tactics that otherwise would prevent black from playing Bf5 as effectively. See chapter X for continuations. } (5. Bd2!! { (0.9@53) White's strongest, 2nd most common response, seen more at low-level despite best practical results at high-level. This develops white's dark-squared bishop, unpinning white's c3 knight, threatening a discovery attack on black's queen, and bringing white closer to castling queen's side. It does however obstruct the queen's defense of the d4 pawn, but the pawn can easily be defended later with Nf3. This doesn't see as good practical results after Bf5, but this is due to white not often playing the best follow-up response, g4, which gives white a theoretical advantage and great practical results. }) (5. Bc4 { White's 3rd most common response, seen more at high-level. This develops the light-squared bishop, putting pressure on the d5 square, the f7 pawn, and bringing white closer to castling king's side. Potentially white's best practical response at all levels, but is the theoretically worse than Bd2, almost to the point of being an inaccuracy. }) (5. Bd3?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This develops the light-squared bishop solidly, putting pressure on the f5 square to potentially trade off light-squared bishops while also brining white closer to castling king's side. However, black can still play Bf5, as the bishop trade reduces the machinery that white has to break open black's position, bringing the game closer to equality. It also obstructs the queen's defense of the d4 pawn, but this can easily be defended with Nf3. } 5... Bf5 6. b4 Qxb4 7. Ne2 Bxd3 8. Qxd3 e6 9. Rb1 Qe7 { White has gambited a pawn in exchange for much greater development, but black argues that white doesn't have the machinery to take advantage of its greater development, leaving white with its initial tempo advantage. }) (5. h3? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This defends the g4 square, preventing a Bg4 pin after white later plays Nf3. It also prepares g4, which discourages black from playing Bf5. However, black can ignore this and play Bf5 anyway as white is neglecting to develop its minor pieces, giving black critical tempo to bring the position back towards equality. } 5... Bf5 6. g4 (6. Bd2 e6 7. g4 Bg6 { White and black have near equal development, and white's king's side attack isn't well supported yet, leaving the position drawish. }) 6... Bg6 7. Bg2 e6 { White and black have near equal development, leaving the position drawish. }) *


