{ White develops its dark-squared bishop with Bd2, unpinning its c3 knight, threatening a discovery attack on black's queen, and bringing white closer to castling queen's side. It does, however, obstruct the queen's defense of the d4 pawn, but the pawn can easily be defended later with Nf3. Black has several possible responses. }
5... Qc7?! { Black's most common response. Inaccuracy. This prophylactically retreats the queen, removing the possibility of a discovered attack on black's queen. However, this is strictly less active than Qb6 as the queen doesn't put any real pressure on white's position, allowing it to develop uncontested. } (5... Qb6! { (0.77@53) Black's 2nd most common, strong response, seen more at low-level. This prophylactically retreats the queen, removing the possibility of a discovered attack, while also forking the b2 and d4 pawns. White can gambit both of these pawns for faster development, so isn't actually concerned with defending the pawns. Therefore, black sees better results at low-level compared to high-level where white is unable to use the won developmental tempo effectively, simply remaining down a pawn. Yet despite this, black can also delay capturing the pawns if they are left unprotected and instead also develop, as the threat can be stronger than its execution. }) (5... Nf6! { (0.79@53) Black's 3rd most common, strong response. This simply develops the king's side knight, putting pressure on the white squares in the center and king's side, particularly d5 and g4, while also bringing black closer to castling king's side. }) (5... Bf5!? { Black's 4th most common response, seen more at high-level. Mistake, but sees good practical results at all levels because its refutation is rarely played. This develops black's dark-squared bishop, putting pressure on the c2 pawn, discouraging white from moving its queen away. However, white can counterattack the light-squared bishop with g4, which helps develop a king's side pawn attack with tempo. See Chapter X for continuations. }) (5... Qd8 { Valencian Variation transposition. Uncommon response. }) *


