{ White develops the king's side knight, defending the d4 pawn again and bringing white closer to castling king's side. However, despite good results at low-level, this sees worse results than Bd2 and Bc4 at high-level, as the f3 knight obstructs several tactics that otherwise would prevent black from playing Bf5 as effectively. }
5... Bf5 (5... Nf6 6. Bd2 (6. h3 Bf5 7. Bd3 (7. g4 Bg6 { Black is able to maintain its bishop on the b1-h7 diagonal since white's f2 pawn is obstructed by the f3 knight. Without the knight placed on f3, black would have been forced to move the bishop off of the diagonal with Be6, retreat the bishop poorly with Bg6, f4, h6, f5 and Bh7, or lock its dark-squared bishop on e7 and prevent easy castling after Bg6, f4, e6, Qe2, and Be7. }) 7... Bxd3 8. Qxd3 { Black argues that white doesn't have the machinery needed to break open its position, leading to a drawn position practically and theoretically. }) 6... Bf5 7. Bc4 (7. Ne4 Qd5 8. Nxf6+ exf6 { Black has similar development to white and can hide its king after castling king's side similarly in function to the main line of the Berlin Defense, creating a drawable or even winnable position practically. Theoretically white still has its initial advantage, however. }) (7. h3 Nbd7 8. g4 Bg6 { Black is able to maintain its bishop on the b1-h7 diagonal since white's f2 pawn is obstructed by the f3 knight. Without the knight placed on f3, black would have been forced to move the bishop off of the diagonal with Be6, retreat the bishop poorly with Bg6, f4, h6, f5 and Bh7, or lock its dark-squared bishop on e7 and prevent easy castling after Bg6, f4, e6, Qe2, and Be7. See later chapters following Bd2 for move order examples. } { [%csl Rf3][%cal Rf2f4] }) 7... e6 (7... Nbd7 8. Qe2 Qc7 9. O-O-O b5 10. Nh4 Bg6 11. Bb3 { Black argues that is has a good defense and an initial queen's side attack in exchange for slightly less development and and uncastled, yet safe, king. This is a slightly better position than it was in after c6, but white still holds its initial advantage. If white wins the bishop pair with Nxg6, black's h8 rook is opened up in exchange. }) 8. Ne4 Qd8 9. Ng3 Bg4 { White has more development and still maintains some advantage, theoretically and practically, but black argues that it has a relatively better positional defense than it had after c6. }) 6. Bc4 (6. Bd2 Nf6 7. Bc4 { Transposition with above. }) (6. Nh4 Nf6 7. Nxf5 Qxf5 { Black sacrifices the bishop pair in exchange for more development, but argues that white doesn't have the machinery to break down black's position. Despite this, white theoretically and practically maintains its initial advantage, though this is still better for black positionally than after c6. }) (6. g4 Bxg4 { White can't pressure away the light-squared bishop with g4, as the knight obstructs defense of the pawn. } { [%csl Rf3][%cal Rd1g4] }) (6. h3 Nf6 7. Nh4 (7. g4 Bg6 { Black is able to maintain its bishop on the b1-h7 diagonal since white's f2 pawn is obstructed by the f3 knight. Without the knight placed on f3, black would have been forced to move the bishop off of the diagonal with Be6, retreat the bishop poorly with Bg6, f4, h6, f5 and Bh7, or lock its dark-squared bishop on e7 and prevent easy castling after Bg6, f4, e6, Qe2, and Be7. See later chapters following Bd2 for move order examples. } { [%csl Rf3][%cal Rf2f4] }) 7... Bg6 8. Nxg6 hxg6 { White has won the bishop pair, but has opened up black's a1 rook and is behind in development, leaving an equal position. }) 6... Nf6 7. Bd2 Nbd7 8. Qe2 Qc7 9. O-O-O e6 { Black argues that is has a good defense in exchange for slightly less development and and uncastled, yet safe, king. This is a slightly better position than it was in after c6, but white still holds an advantage. } *


