{ Black counterattack's white's pawn in the center with e5, threatening to trade and weaken white's control of the center. This can see decent results at very low-level due to several simple tactics at black's disposal; however, with good play white can ignore this and simply develop with Nf3. }
5. Nf3!! { (1.40@50) Goteborg System. White's strongest, 2nd most common response. This defends the d4 pawn again, pressures the e5 pawn, and brings white closer to castling king's side. Black has several tactical options it can attempt in order to gain an advantage, but is otherwise positionally down at least one pawn. } (5. dxe5? { White's 2nd most common response. Mistake. This attempts to begin a pawn trade on white's terms, opening up the center. However, black can intermezzo with Bb4, double attacking white's c3 knight and functionally forcing white to obstruct its own queen with Bd2, continuously develop while defending the c3 knight with the queen, or else give up severe damage to its pawn structure after Bxc3 and bxc3. } 5... Bb4 6. Bd2 (6. Nf3 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 { Destroyed queen's side pawn structure. }) 6... Qxe5+ { Obstructed queen and tempo attack on white's king. }) (5. d5) (5. Qe2) 5... Bg4 { Collijin Variation. } (5... exd4) (5... e4) (5... Bb4) *


