{ Black retreats its queen to its original, secure position with Qd8, maintaining pressure on the d-file and arguing that white's extra development is negated by its slightly more exposed king. White has several possible responses. }
4. d4! { White's most common, strong response. This establishes a pawn in the board center, controlling it and opening up both the queen and dark-squared bishop. } (4. Nf3!? { White's 2nd most common, strong response, seen more at low-level. This develops the king's side knight, preparing white to castle king's side. Despite its theoretical strength, this response doesn't see much play at high-level, likely due to the practical difficulty of playing this position after the f3 knight is pinned to the queen by black with Bg4. }) (4. Bc4? { White's 3rd most common response, seen more at low-level. Mistake. This develops the light-squared bishop in preparation to castle king's side. However, this gives black time to gain control of the d4 square with c5, preventing white from immediately playing d4 and allowing black to later develop its queen's side knight without obstructing its c-pawn. }) (4. g3? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This prepares to fianchetto the light-squared bishop which will put pressure on the major a8-h1 diagonal and bring white closer to castling king's side. However, this allows black to play c5, gaining control of the d4 square and preventing white from playing d4, hindering its developmental potential and leading to a more drawish game. }) (4. h3? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This defends the g4 square, preventing black from pinning white's knight to its queen after Nc3 with Bg4. However, this doesn't control the center or develop a minor piece, essentially giving up the initial tempo/developmental advantage that white had, creating an equal position. }) (4. d3? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This solidly, yet passively controls some of the center and opens up white's dark-squared bishop. However, this doesn't control as much of the center as d4 or develop a minor piece, essentially giving up the initial tempo/developmental advantage that white had, creating an equal position. }) *


