{ White establishes a pawn in the board center with d4, controlling the center and opening up both its queen and dark-squared bishop. }
4... Nf6! { Black's most common, strong response. This develops the king's side knight, defending some key central squares and the g4 square, possibly enabling Ng4 or Bg4 tactics if white plays poorly. It also prepares to castle king's side. } (4... c6?! { Black's 2nd most common response, seen more at low-level. Inaccuracy. This solidly defends the d5 square, preventing white from ever effectively advancing its pawn with d5. It also defends the king from any bishop attacks with Bb5+. However, this obstructs the normal development of black's queen's side knight, forcing it to develop with Nd7 or Na6, prevents black from ever effectively castling queen's side, and continues to neglect developing any minor pieces, giving white an even larger developmental advantage. }) (4... e6!? { Black's 3rd most common, strong response. Theoretically sound, but rarely sees play at high-level and practically sees worse results at all levels, possibly due to difficulties recovering from a developmental disadvantage. This defends the d5 square, preventing white from advancing its d-pawn without a pawn trade. This also opens up black's queen and dark-squared bishop, bringing black closer to castling king's side. }) (4... Bf5?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This develops the light-squared bishop, targeting the c2 pawn and preventing white from easily moving its queen, which defends the pawn. However, white can either commit to castling queen's side by first tempo attacking the light-squared bishop with g4 or even trade away the pawn by forking the light-squared bishop and b7 pawn with Qf3, forcing the light-squared bishop to retreat or massively blunder with Bxc2. }) (4... g6! { Uncommon, strong response. This prepares to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop, which will put additional pressure on the d4 pawn and the rest of the major a1-h8 diagonal in addition to bringing black closer to castling king's side. }) (4... Nc6?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This develops the queen's side knight, attempting to put pressure on the d4 pawn. However, this allows white to tempo attack the c6 knight with d5, moving the pawn to a more easily defended space and forcing the c6 knight to move away. }) *


