{ Black develops its king's side knight, defending some key central squares and the g4 square, possibly enabling Ng4 or Bg4 tactics if white plays poorly. It also prepares black to castle king's side. White has several possible responses. }
5. Nf3! { White's strongest, most common response. This simply develops the king's side knight, defending the d4 pawn and preparing white to castle king's side. White argues that any Bg4 pin of the f3 knight onto the queen is ineffective with black's best option to lose the bishop pair by trading its light-squared bishop for the knight. } (5. Bc4?! { White's 2nd most common response. Inaccuracy. This aggressively develops the light-squared bishop, putting pressure on the f7 pawn and preparing white to castle king's side. However, this slightly overextends the light-squared bishop, allowing black to pressure it with a6 and b5 for tempo development of a queen's side pawn attack. }) (5. Bg5?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This attempts to pin the f6 knight to its e7 pawn and queen, discouraging black from advancing its e-pawn. However, black can simply push away the bishop to f4 with h6, making the response strictly worse than Bf4 initially. }) (5. h3? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This defends the g4 square, preventing any Ng4 attacks or Bg4 pins after white plays Nf3. However, white doesn't need to preemptively stop Bg4 and should instead simply react to it with h3 afterwards. By doing so now, this wastes critical tempo that black can use to fortify its position with c6 or e6. }) (5. Bf4!? { Uncommon response. This develops the dark-squared bishop to the only active square where it doesn't obstruct the queen (d2) or be easily attacked (e3 and g5). This helps prepare white to castle queen's side while also pressuring black's queen's side. This response doesn't see great practical results at all levels, but this is more due to white players choosing not to follow the most optimal developmental patterns rather than the lines being difficult for white. Because of this, Bf4 may be a good sideline for further study, but it is still slightly theoretically inferior to Nf3 overall. }) (5. Be3?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This develops the dark-squared bishop, adding another defender to the d4 pawn and preparing white to castle queen's side. However, this response requires difficult/obtuse positional maneuvers like Qf3 in order to maintain only a slight theoretical advantage for white that is still worse for white compared to Nf3 (and slightly worse than Bf4). The line may be worth studying due to its complexity, but it seems one could better spend their time following the Nf3 mainline (or Bf4). }) *


