{ White simply develops its king's side knight with Nf3, defending the d4 pawn and preparing white to castle king's side. }
5... Bg4!? { Black's most common response. Inaccuracy, but sees good practical results at all levels as at least a drawing technique, likely due to the difficulty for white to break black's solid position. This pins the f3 knight to the queen, discouraging white from moving the knight. However, white can simply push away the bishop or force it to trade (losing the bishop pair) with h3. Black can't easily retreat with Bh5 without giving up a lot of positional advantage or the bishop pair after g4 and Ne5. Despite this, trading away black's light-squared bishop for white's f3 knight with Bxf3 is very drawish and therefore can be seen as a good result for black. } (5... e6!? { Black's strongest, 2nd most common response, seen more at low-level. This defends the d5 square, preventing white from effectively playing d5. This also opens up the dark-squared bishop, bringing black closer to castling king's side. Despite its theoretical strength, this response sees poor practical results at all levels, likely due to the difficulty of play after a Bg5 pin of black's f6 knight on the queen or Bc4. }) (5... c6! { Ilundain Variation. Black's 3rd most common, strong response. This solidly defends the d5 square, preventing white from ever effectively advancing its pawn with d5. It also defends the king from any bishop attacks with Bb5+. This all comes at a slight cost of preventing black from ever effectively castling queen's side. }) (5... Bf5?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This develops the light-squared bishop, targeting the c2 pawn and preventing white from easily moving its queen, which defends the pawn. However, this doesn't have any immediate threats, allows white to centralize its knight with Ne5, and can be tempo attacked with g4 (after Ne5) to begin a king's side pawn attack or fianchetto the light-squared bishop. }) (5... g6! { Uncommon, strong response, seen slightly more at high-level. This prepares to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop, putting pressure on the d4 pawn, c3 knight, and the rest of the major a1-h8 diagonal, while also bringing black closer to castling king's side. }) (5... Nc6?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This develops black's queen's side knight, putting additional pressure on the d4 pawn. However, this allows white to pin the c6 knight to the king with Bf5, trading the bishop for the knight and damaging black's pawn structure after a6, as white's developmental advantage, black's loss of tempo from playing a6, and black's damaged pawn structure makes up for losing the bishop pair. }) (5... a6! { Uncommon, strong response. This simply defends the b5 square from any knight attacks with Nb5 or bishop checks or pins with Bb5. This is essentially a waiting move, preparing to develop in response to white. }) *


