{ Black pins white's f3 knight to its queen, discouraging white from moving the knight. This line is theoretically dubious, but it is the most common response and practically sees good results at all levels as at least a drawing technique for black. White has a few responses. }
6. Be2? { White's most common response, seen more at low-level. Mistake. This interferes with black's pin on the f3 knight, allowing white to move either its queen or knight. This also brings white closer to castling king's side. However, this waste a critical tempo, allowing black to more easily develop and defend its position with either c6 or e6 instead of forcing the Bxf3 trade immediately after h3. } (6. h3! { White's strongest, 2nd most common response, seen more at high-level. This immediately pressures black's light-squared bishop, forcing it to retreat or trade for the f3 knight, losing the bishop pair. }) (6. Bc4? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This develops the light-squared bishop, putting pressure on the f7 pawn and bringing white closer to castling king's side. However, this waste a critical tempo, allowing black to more easily develop and defend its position with e6 instead of forcing the Bxf3 trade immediately after h3. }) (6. Be3?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This defends the d4 pawn, bringing white closer to castling queen's side. However, this waste a critical tempo, allowing black to more easily develop and defend its position with e6 instead of forcing the Bxf3 trade immediately after h3. }) (6. Bf4?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This develops the dark-squared bishop to the only active square where it doesn't obstruct the queen (d2) or be easily attacked (e3 and g5). This helps prepare white to castle queen's side while also pressuring black's queen's side. However, this waste a critical tempo, allowing black to more easily develop and defend its position with e6 instead of forcing the Bxf3 trade immediately after h3. }) *


