{ White immediately pressures black's light-squared bishop, forcing it to retreat or trade for the f3 knight, losing the bishop pair. Black essentially has a few forced responses since the light-squared bishop is pressured. }
6... Bxf3! { Black's strongest, most common response. This trades the light-squared bishop for white's f3 knight. Black argues that sacrificing the bishop pair is worth winning back some developmental equality and a tempo from white playing a3, as white doesn't have the material and/or positional strength to break black's defenses, leading to more drawn positions at higher levels. } (6... Bh5?? { Black's 2nd most common response. Slight blunder. This retreats the light-squared bishop, maintaining the pin on the f3 knight. However, white can interfere with the pin with g4, tempo attacking the light-squared bishop and forcing it to g6, which white can pressure with Ne5. This essentially still loses the bishop pair, but allows white to develop a king's side pawn attack with tempo, possibly fianchetto its light-squared bishop with tempo, and centralize its knight with the threat of winning the bishop pair, the threat of which can be used to force black to develop inefficiently. }) (6... Bf5?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This retreats the light-squared bishop, putting pressure on the c2 pawn. This prevents white's queen from easily moving away from defense of it. However, white can continue its king's side pawn attack by tempo attacking the light-squared bishop with g4, forcing it to again retreat. }) 7. Qxf3! { White's strongest, most common response. This simply recaptures black's light-squared bishop and puts pressure on the b7 pawn. This appears to hang the d4 pawn, but black can't capture it due to Qxb7 which wins both a pawn and the rook. } (7. gxf3?? { Uncommon response, seen only at low-level. Slight blunder. This attempts to maintain defense of the d4 pawn by capturing with the g-pawn, opening up the g-file for white's rook. However, this damages white's pawn structure, prevents white from castling king's side, and the d4 pawn could have been tactically defended with Qxf3, anyway. }) 7... c6! { Black's strongest, most common response. This defends the b7 pawn by obstructing white's queen, defends the b5 square and obstructs any Nb5 knight attacks or Bb5 pins/checks, and defends the d5 square, preventing white from ever effectively advancing its d-pawn. } (7... Nc6?? { Uncommon response. Blunder. This attempts to develop black's queen's side knight to obstruct the white queen's pressure on the b7 pawn. However, white can simply pin the black knight to its king with Bb5. Black can't prevent white from capturing the knight. If black recaptures with the pawn, white can fork the king and rook with Qxc6, and if black tries to defend the knight with the queen with Qd7, white can attack the knight with d5. }) *


