{ Black trades its light-squared bishop for white's f3 knight, causing white to recapture with the queen, forcing black to obstruct the queen's pressure on the b7 pawn with c7. White now has several options. }
8. Be3! { White's most common, strong response. This simply develops the dark-squared bishop, defending the d4 pawn and bringing white closer to castling queen's side. } (8. Bf4!? { White's 2nd most common response. Inaccuracy with best play, but sets a non-obvious trap. Firstly, this develops the dark-squared bishop, putting pressure on the b8 knight and defending the c7 square, bringing white closer to possibly castling queen's side. More importantly, this sets a trap for black by tactically sacrificing the d4 pawn. Black can't recapture without allowing white to fork the queen and c7 square with Nb5. If black captures the b2 pawn in response, threatening to win the a1 rook, white can still play Nc7+, forking the black king and rook, as black is forced to play Kd8 which let's white intermezzo with Rd1+, saving the rook. However, black can simply avoid capturing the pawn and play e6 instead, preventing black from being forced to play Kd8 and therefore avoiding the trap line. }) (8. Bg5?? { White's 3rd most common response, seen more at low-level. Slight blunder. This attempts to put pressure on the f6 knight, discouraging black from moving its e7 pawn and bringing white closer to castling king's side. However, this simply hangs the d4 pawn without any tempo compensation from Be3, as white has already moved the bishop once, while also preventing white from easily castling queen's side. }) (8. g4!? { White's uncommon, strong response. This aggressively advances one of king's side pawns, enabling white to fianchetto its light-squared bishop, threatening to attack black's f6 knight with g5, and bringing white's pawns closer to a king's side pawn attack. This comes at the cost of gambiting the d4 pawn for tempo development and weakening the effectiveness of castling king's side, especially considering how castling queen's side may be difficult after Qxd4. Despite its theoretical and practical strength, this response is not seen much at all levels as white players often prefer to avoid gambiting the d4 pawn, instead defending with Be3, which is equivalently strong. This may be a good line to further study at high-level to avoid theory. }) (8. Qd3!? { Uncommon response, seen more at high-level, likely due to attempts to avoid main line drawish positions. Inaccuracy, but sees good practical results at all levels. This repositions the queen, defending the d4 pawn, forming a battery on the a6-f1 diagonal, and preparing white to later play d5 to break open black's position. However, this moves the queen a second time, giving up some of the developmental advantage that white had. }) (8. Bc4?? { Uncommon response, seen more at low-level. Slight blunder. This attempts to develop the light-squared bishop, threatening the f7 pawn and preventing black from moving its f6 knight easily. However, this simply hangs the d4 pawn to Qxd4, which tempo attacks the bishop and forces it to move again. }) *


