{ White simply develops the dark-squared bishop with Be3, defending the d4 pawn and bringing white closer to castling queen's side. }
8... e6! { Black's strongest, most common response. This opens up the dark-squared bishop, bringing black closer to castling king's side. It also defends the d5 pawn again, further discouraging white from ever playing d5. This comes at the cost of potentially enabling a Bg5 pin on the f6 knight later in the game, though this can be mitigated by Be7 in response. } (8... Nbd7?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This develops the queen's side knight, defending the other f6 knight and preparing to reroute it to b6. However, this is slow and allows white tempo to continue its development by castling queen's side. }) (8... Nd5?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This centralizes black's knight, putting pressure on the e3 bishop and threatening to win the bishop pair or trade knights. However, white can simply move the bishop now that the d5 knight obstructs the black queen's pressure on the d4 pawn. }) (8... g6? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This prepares to fianchetto black's dark-squared bishop, bringing black closer to castling king's side. However, this is very slow and gives white time to castle queen's side and organize its structure, giving a large positional advantage as black's king's side now has pawn hooks for a pawn attack. }) *


